/// \defgroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io The File base class and in-memory files
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief File, the abstract open file that the engine's file system hands
///        out, and the two BFME classes that read from and write to a
///        memory buffer instead of a disk file.
///
/// Files opened through FileSystem::openFile are objects of a File
/// subclass, used through File's virtual members: a loose file on disk
/// (LocalFile), a file held whole in memory (RAMFile, which can be filled
/// from a `.big` archive entry) or a file read from an archive on demand
/// (StreamingArchiveFile). Callers normally get one from
/// FileSystem::openFile and never name the subclass;
/// \ref sub_common_services_files describes the file system that hands them
/// out and the search order between loose files and archives. BFME adds two
/// File subclasses over a block of memory, MemoryReadFile and
/// MemoryWriteFile, which their callers create through factory functions
/// rather than through the file system.
///
/// Not every file the game touches goes through File:
/// - In Zero Hour, the file-backed Xfer classes (XferSave, XferLoad and
///   XferDeepCRC, the subclass of XferCRC that writes its data to a file)
///   and GameStateMap open their files with `fopen`, and the Bink video player
///   passes a path straight to `BinkOpen`. How BFME 2 opens save games and
///   videos is not established here.
/// - In BFME 2, RecorderClass::startRecording creates the replay file with
///   `_wfopen`.
/// - WWLib's own file classes (FileClass, with RawFileClass among its
///   subclasses) are a separate hierarchy, also present in BFME 2.
///
/// This group covers:
/// - **File's own members**: the constructor and destructor, File::open and
///   File::close, the helpers built on File's pure virtual `seek`
///   (File::size, File::position, File::eof), File::print, and the File::lock
///   and File::unlock pair that BFME added. Subclasses inherit these or call
///   up to them; LocalFile::open, `RAMFile::open(File *)` and the
///   `openFromArchive` members of RAMFile and StreamingArchiveFile all call
///   File::open. RAMFile's other `open`, which takes a name and access
///   flags, reaches File::open only through `RAMFile::open(File *)`.
/// - **In-memory files**: the MemoryWriteFile constructor and
///   MemoryWriteFile::write, and createMemoryReadFile, which wraps a buffer
///   in a MemoryReadFile. MemoryReadFile's own members and MemoryWriteFile's
///   other members are not covered here.
///
/// \par Where the code comes from
/// - File is Zero Hour's `Common/System/File.cpp` class with BFME changes:
///   a mutex handle with File::lock and File::unlock, a plain virtual delete
///   instead of Zero Hour's memory-pool release in File::close, and a
///   destructor that cannot delete the object twice. BFME 1 already has all
///   of these, and the BFME 2 bodies behave as BFME 1's do.
/// - MemoryReadFile and MemoryWriteFile are not in Zero Hour. The project
///   took them from the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction (its `File.cpp`).
/// - The assert strings in the WorldBuilder debug build give the original
///   BFME 2 source files: File lived in `Libraries/Source/file/File.cpp` and
///   the two memory files in `Libraries/Source/file/file_memoryfile.cpp`,
///   not in Zero Hour's `GameEngine/Source/Common/System/`.
///
/// \par Access flags
/// File::open and File::print test these bits of the access value. The
/// values are Zero Hour's (`Common/file.h`); BFME 2's File::open tests the
/// same bits.
///
/// | Flag | Value | Meaning |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `READ` | 1 | open for reading |
/// | `WRITE` | 2 | open for writing |
/// | `APPEND` | 4 | seek to the end on open |
/// | `TRUNCATE` | 16 | discard the existing contents on open |
/// | `TEXT` | 32 | text data; File::print requires it |
/// | `BINARY` | 64 | binary data; File::open's default |
/// | `STREAMING` | 256 | read on demand rather than into memory; never with `WRITE` |
///
/// The meanings paraphrase the comments in Zero Hour's header. The remarks
/// that `BINARY` is the default and that File::print requires `TEXT` come
/// from the File::open and File::print bodies, and File::open is also what
/// refuses `STREAMING` with `WRITE`.
///
/// Zero Hour's header also defines `NONE` (0), `CREATE` (8), `ONLYNEW`
/// (128) and the combination `READWRITE` (`READ` with `WRITE`). File::open
/// neither tests nor sets `CREATE` or `ONLYNEW`; what the subclasses do
/// with them in BFME 2 is not covered here.
///
/// \par How the memory files are used
/// The two classes are a pair for turning an object into bytes and back
/// through the Xfer system without touching the disk. Among the callers
/// traced here:
/// - **Writing.** A small factory function, whose original name is not
///   known, creates each MemoryWriteFile. The caller opens an XferSave on
///   it, lets the object transfer itself, then takes the finished buffer
///   and its length out of the file through a further member, whose
///   original name and class are not known either. That member clears the
///   file's buffer fields and then makes the virtual `close` call.
///   MemoryWriteFile inherits File::close (its virtual table holds File's
///   own body), so the file is renamed `<no file>` and marked closed; it is
///   not destroyed, because the constructor never sets delete-on-close.
///   From then on the caller owns the buffer and must release it, and the
///   callers do not all release it the same way:
///   - ConnectionManager packs Create-a-Hero data into the network command
///     it sends to the other players, and RecorderClass::startRecording
///     stores each player's Create-a-Hero data in a replay. Both release
///     the buffer with `delete[]`.
///   - The GameSpy persistent-storage code packs a player's statistics (as
///     "playerStats"). PSThreadClass::Thread_Function releases those
///     buffers through the game allocator's `free` wrapper, and the
///     function that saves a cached copy of the statistics in the GameSpy
///     preferences releases them with a scalar `delete`.
///   - GameLogic::update creates one for one of its CRC check modes. A
///     function that records per-frame CRC results, whose name is not
///     confirmed, is handed such files and keeps a list of them; once the
///     list passes a limit, it takes the buffer out of the oldest and
///     releases it through the same `free` wrapper.
/// - **Reading.** createMemoryReadFile wraps a received buffer, and an Xfer
///   loader reads the object back: ConnectionManager::processHero does
///   this for the hero command's data, and the GameSpy persistent-storage
///   code does it for the statistics.
///
/// Which allocator a MemoryWriteFile's buffer comes from is not established
/// (see MemoryWriteFile::write), so this page does not say which of the
/// releases above matches it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Mods never deal with these classes directly. Data the engine loads
///   through FileSystem::openFile can come loose or from inside a `.big`
///   file, and reaches the code as a File; which subclass serves it depends
///   on where the file system finds it (see \ref sub_common_services_files).
/// - Files opened outside the file system (see the list above) do not go
///   through that search. In Zero Hour those paths (the file-backed Xfer
///   classes XferSave, XferLoad and XferDeepCRC, GameStateMap and the Bink
///   player) hand a path name to `fopen` or `BinkOpen`, which look for it on
///   disk, not inside a `.big`.
///   Check how BFME 2 opens a given kind of file before moving it into an
///   archive.
/// - MemoryWriteFile adds no header, length or checksum of its own: it holds
///   exactly the bytes written to it. The callers write through an XferSave,
///   so the buffer holds XferSave's stream format: a short header written
///   when the XferSave is opened (two tag words, a number the caller
///   supplies and a flag word), block bookkeeping (a marker and a
///   placeholder at the start of each block, which is filled in with the
///   block's end offset when the block ends), and the object's own Xfer
///   output. When the flag word is set the stream carries more besides,
///   including a marker word at the end of each block, extra data between
///   a block's start marker and its placeholder (in BFME 1 a short record
///   naming the block; BFME 2's writer for it is not established) and an
///   extra word ahead of some values. The hero and statistics writers
///   traced here leave the flag off. On the reading side
///   the loader's open step fails on a buffer whose header tags it does not
///   recognise. The Create-a-Hero blob in a hero command or a replay is
///   therefore XferSave's stream wrapped around the hero data's Xfer output:
///   changing either the hero data's Xfer code or XferSave's stream format
///   changes the network and replay format, and builds that differ in
///   either cannot be assumed to read each other's hero data.
/// - File::print works only on a file opened with `TEXT`. Files are binary
///   unless the opener asks otherwise, and a MemoryWriteFile is created
///   with `BINARY` access.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Sizes and positions are 32-bit signed `Int` values throughout: File
///   cannot describe a file of 2 GB or more, and File::size reports 0 when
///   the subclass's seek fails.
/// - File::lock and File::unlock are a Win32 seam: they use a kernel mutex
///   (`CreateMutexA`, `WaitForSingleObject`, `ReleaseMutex`, closed with
///   `CloseHandle` in the destructor). A Win32 mutex lets the thread that
///   owns it lock again without blocking, so a portable replacement must be
///   a recursive mutex.
/// - File::print formats with `vsprintf` into a fixed 10 KB stack buffer and
///   checks the length only afterwards. A port should use a bounded
///   formatter.
/// - File::close releases a delete-on-close file with the global `delete`
///   operator through the virtual destructor, where Zero Hour returned it
///   to File's memory pool. Where BFME 2's global allocator gets its memory
///   is outside this group (see \ref sub_memory).
/// - A MemoryWriteFile's buffer is grown through an allocation helper whose
///   identity is not established, and the callers traced here release the
///   buffer through three different routines: array `delete[]`, scalar
///   `delete`, and the game allocator's `free` wrapper. A port that changes
///   the allocators must check every release of such a buffer.


/// \file Common/System/File_createMemoryReadFile.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief createMemoryReadFile, the factory that wraps a memory block in a
///        MemoryReadFile.
///
/// The body is ported from the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction's `File.cpp`,
/// and so are the class declarations the unit compiles against.


/// \class File
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief The abstract open file: name, access mode, open state and the
///        virtual read, write and seek interface every File subclass
///        implements.
///
/// Zero Hour's header describes File as the interface all code should use
/// in place of its subclasses, with the file system creating the objects.
/// BFME 2 keeps that design. The subclasses supply `read`, `write`, `seek`,
/// the text scanners (`nextLine`, `scanInt`, `scanReal`, `scanString`),
/// `readEntireAndClose` and `convertToRAMFile`; in BFME 2 File's own
/// virtual table has no body for those, so File itself cannot be
/// instantiated. File implements the rest, listed in
/// \ref grp_w1018_file_memory_io.
///
/// \par State
/// - The file name (Zero Hour reads it back through `getName`). File::File
///   and File::close set it to `<no file>`, File::open to the name it is
///   given, and the memory-file constructors to a label of their own
///   (`<MemoryReadFile>`, or the caller's label or `<MemoryWriteFile>`).
///   File::open stores the name before it checks the flags, so a file whose
///   open was refused stays closed under the new name.
/// - The access flags File::open settled on (see the table in
///   \ref grp_w1018_file_memory_io).
/// - An open flag, and a delete-on-close flag.
/// - BFME only: the handle of the mutex behind File::lock and File::unlock,
///   created on first use.
///
/// \par Lifetime
/// A File is created by the file system or a factory, opened, used and
/// closed. When the delete-on-close flag is set, File::close also destroys
/// the object, so the caller's pointer is invalid after the call; the
/// flag is how a caller hands the object's lifetime to its close. Callers
/// set the flag through the inline `deleteOnClose` member of Zero Hour's
/// header; in BFME 2, for example, GameTextTable::readSTR and
/// GameTextTable::readCSF set it on the file they open. The memory-file
/// constructors do not set it.
///
/// \par Thread safety
/// File::lock and File::unlock give each File its own mutex, which BFME
/// added. Nothing in File's other members takes it; a caller that shares a
/// File between threads must bracket its own calls. Which callers do so is
/// not covered here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Data a mod supplies that the engine loads through FileSystem::openFile
/// is read through this interface, whichever subclass serves it. Some
/// files are opened another way; the group page
/// (\ref grp_w1018_file_memory_io) lists the cases traced there.


/// \class MemoryWriteFile
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief A write-only File that collects everything written to it in a
///        heap buffer it grows itself.
///
/// BFME added the class; Zero Hour has none. A MemoryWriteFile is open from
/// construction, with `WRITE` and `BINARY` access, and is never opened by
/// name. Writes go to the current position, overwriting what is there, and
/// extend the size when they run past the end; seeks move within what has
/// been written. The callers traced here use it as the target of an
/// XferSave and then take the buffer out of it; the group page
/// (\ref grp_w1018_file_memory_io) lists them.
///
/// \par State
/// - The buffer, its capacity, the number of bytes written (the file's
///   size) and the current position. The buffer starts empty and is
///   allocated by the first write; MemoryWriteFile::write describes how it
///   grows.
/// - A second string member, constructed empty, whose purpose is not known.
///   Apart from the destructor releasing it, no BFME 1 member uses it.
///
/// BFME 1's MemoryWriteFile answers `size` and `position` from its own
/// counters instead of seeking, and its `read`, `open` and
/// `readEntireAndClose` always fail. Those BFME 2 members are not covered
/// here; where this page lists them, the text shown is File's.
///
/// \par Ownership
/// Until a caller takes the buffer out (see the group page), only the file
/// holds it; after that the file no longer refers to it, and the caller
/// owns it and must release it. The callers traced here do not agree on
/// how: the hero-command and replay code use `delete[]`, while the
/// persistent-storage and CRC code use the game allocator's `free` wrapper
/// or a scalar `delete` (the group page gives each case). The allocator the
/// buffer comes from is not established. BFME 1's destructor frees a buffer
/// that was never taken; BFME 2's destructor is not covered here.
/// MemoryWriteFile does not override `close`: its virtual table holds
/// File::close. The constructor does not set the delete-on-close flag, so
/// closing a MemoryWriteFile does not destroy it.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name and member layout come from the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction,
/// where the class is named by the `<MemoryWriteFile>` placeholder its
/// constructor gives the file. The BFME 2 constructor uses the same string,
/// and the WorldBuilder debug build's assert strings name
/// MemoryWriteFile::write.


/// \fn File::File(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Constructs a closed file with no access mode and the placeholder
///        name `<no file>`.
///
/// Only subclasses construct a File: the MemoryReadFile, MemoryWriteFile
/// and RAMFile constructors, among others, call it first. It clears the
/// access flags, the open and delete-on-close flags and, in BFME, the mutex
/// handle, then sets the name.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's constructor is the same apart from the mutex handle,
///       which Zero Hour does not have.
/// \see File::~File, File::open


/// \fn File::~File(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Closes the file if it is open and frees the mutex File::lock
///        created.
///
/// The subclass destructors (RAMFile and LocalFile among them) end here, as
/// does the deleting destructor that File::close reaches through the
/// virtual table.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The destructor clears the delete-on-close flag before it calls
/// File::close, so closing a file that is already being destroyed cannot
/// delete it a second time. It then closes the mutex handle if File::lock
/// ever created one.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's destructor only calls `close`, and does not clear the
///       flag first. BFME 1 and BFME 2 add the guard and the handle
///       release.
/// \see File::close, File::lock


/// \fn Bool File::open(const Char *filename, Int access)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Records the name and access mode and marks the file open; the
///        base step that the subclasses' `open` members call.
///
/// File::open touches no file. The subclasses call it and do the real work
/// themselves: LocalFile::open opens the disk file, `RAMFile::open(File *)`
/// copies another open file into memory, and the `openFromArchive` members
/// of RAMFile and StreamingArchiveFile load or locate the data in an
/// archive. RAMFile's other `open`, which takes a name and access flags,
/// reaches File::open only through `RAMFile::open(File *)`. Zero Hour's
/// comments on this function say that a subclass must call it first, and
/// must call File::close if its own open then fails.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Fails if the file is already open.
/// - Stores the name before checking the flags, so a rejected open still
///   renames the file.
/// - Rejects `STREAMING` with `WRITE`, and `TEXT` with `BINARY`.
/// - Fills in defaults: `READ` when neither `READ` nor `WRITE` is given,
///   `TRUNCATE` when neither `READ` nor `APPEND` is given (a plain write
///   replaces the file), and `BINARY` when neither `TEXT` nor `BINARY` is
///   given.
/// - Stores the result and sets the open flag.
///
/// \param filename  Name to record. File::open only stores it.
/// \param access    Access flags (see the table in
///                  \ref grp_w1018_file_memory_io). Zero Hour's header
///                  declares 0 as the default, which becomes `READ` and
///                  `BINARY`.
/// \return `TRUE` when the file is now open; `FALSE` when it was already
///         open or the flags conflict.
///
/// \note The logic is Zero Hour's, unchanged in BFME 1 and BFME 2.
/// \see File::close


/// \fn void File::close(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Marks the file closed, restores the placeholder name and, when
///        the delete-on-close flag is set, destroys the object.
///
/// Zero Hour's header warns that the File object is no longer valid after
/// this call. That holds whenever the delete-on-close flag is set.
///
/// Its callers include RAMFile::close, File::~File and the RAMFile,
/// StreamingArchiveFile and LocalFile destructors, which call it directly.
/// LocalFile::close and StreamingArchiveFile::close end in a jump to it, as
/// in Zero Hour, and MemoryReadFile and MemoryWriteFile do not override
/// `close`: their virtual tables hold File::close itself.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Does nothing if the file is not open. Otherwise it renames the file to
/// `<no file>` and clears the open flag. If delete-on-close is set, it
/// clears that flag and deletes the object through its virtual destructor.
///
/// \note Zero Hour returns the object to its memory pool (File derives from
///       MemoryPoolObject there) and leaves the flag set. BFME 1 and BFME 2
///       use an ordinary delete and clear the flag first.
/// \warning After a delete-on-close file is closed, its pointer dangles.
/// \see File::open, File::~File


/// \fn Int File::size(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Returns the file's length; File's own version finds it by
///        seeking to the end and back.
///
/// The default implementation for subclasses that do not keep a size of
/// their own; in BFME 2, LocalFile, RAMFile and StreamingArchiveFile all
/// inherit it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Remembers the current position, seeks 0 bytes from the end to get the
/// length, then seeks back to the remembered position. Three calls to the
/// subclass's `seek`.
///
/// \return The length in bytes, or 0 if the seek to the end fails.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's body, unchanged in BFME 1 and BFME 2.
/// \see File::position, File::eof


/// \fn Int File::position(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Returns the current read or write position; File's own version
///        asks for it by seeking 0 bytes from the current position.
///
/// The default for subclasses that do not track the position themselves;
/// they inherit it.
///
/// \return The position in bytes from the start, or whatever the
///         subclass's `seek` returns on failure (-1 in Zero Hour's
///         contract).
///
/// \see File::size, File::eof


/// \fn Bool File::print(const Char *format, ...)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Writes a printf-style formatted string to a text-mode file.
///
/// It is virtual and every File subclass inherits it, so callers normally
/// reach it through the virtual table; which code calls it is not covered
/// here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Fails unless the access flags include `TEXT`. Formats with `vsprintf`
/// into a 10 KB buffer on the stack, refuses a result that fills the
/// buffer, and writes the text through the subclass's `write`.
///
/// \param format  A `printf` format string, followed by its arguments.
/// \return `TRUE` when the whole string was written; `FALSE` when the file
///         is not in text mode, the text was too long, or the write was
///         short.
///
/// \warning The length check comes after `vsprintf` has already written
///          the text, so a result longer than the buffer overruns the
///          stack before it is refused. The WorldBuilder debug build
///          asserts at that point, as Zero Hour does.
/// \see File::open


/// \fn Bool File::eof(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Tells whether the current position is at the end of the file.
///
/// A non-virtual helper. Its callers include
/// FileSystem::initialiseRestrictedFilelist, AptAnimData::createRenderData
/// and LadderList::checkLadder.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Compares File::position with File::size, through the virtual table, so
/// a subclass's own versions are used when it has them. With the inherited
/// versions one call costs four seeks.
///
/// \return `TRUE` when the position equals the size.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's body, unchanged in BFME 1 and BFME 2.
/// \see File::position, File::size


/// \fn void File::lock(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Takes the file's own mutex, creating it on first use.
///
/// BFME added the pair File::lock and File::unlock; Zero Hour has neither.
/// They are virtual and every File subclass inherits them, so callers
/// normally reach them through the virtual table; which code calls them is
/// not covered here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The first call creates a Win32 mutex already owned by the calling
/// thread. Later calls wait for it with no time limit.
///
/// \warning The check and the creation are not protected: two threads that
///          lock a never-locked file at the same moment can each create a
///          mutex, and one handle is then lost. This follows from the body;
///          whether any caller can hit it is not established.
/// \par Remastering
/// A Win32 mutex is recursive for the owning thread, so a replacement must
/// let the same thread lock again without blocking and must count releases.
/// \see File::unlock


/// \fn void File::unlock(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Releases the mutex File::lock took, if one was ever created.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls `ReleaseMutex` on the handle; a file that was never locked has no
/// handle and the call is skipped. The handle itself stays open until
/// File::~File.
///
/// \see File::lock


/// \fn MemoryWriteFile::MemoryWriteFile(const char *name)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Creates an empty write-only memory file that is already open.
///
/// It is reached through a small factory function (see the group page),
/// whose callers pass labels such as "heroString" and "playerStats".
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Runs File::File, zeroes the buffer pointer, size, position and capacity,
/// constructs the second string member empty, marks the file open with
/// `WRITE` and `BINARY` access, and names it after `name`, or
/// `<MemoryWriteFile>` when `name` is null. No buffer is allocated until the
/// first MemoryWriteFile::write.
///
/// \param name  Label for the file, used only as its name. May be null.
///
/// \note The behaviour is the same as BFME 1's constructor.
/// \see MemoryWriteFile::write, File::File


/// \fn Int MemoryWriteFile::write(const void *buffer, Int bytes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_file_memory_io
/// \brief Copies bytes into the memory buffer at the current position,
///        growing the buffer when needed.
///
/// The override of File's `write`, reached through the virtual table. An
/// XferSave opened on the file writes through it, and XferSave::endBlock
/// seeks back to patch each block's header, so a write can land inside
/// what has already been written. File::print would also use it, but a
/// MemoryWriteFile is created without `TEXT`, so File::print refuses it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A negative count, or a positive count with a null buffer, is an error.
///   A null buffer or a zero count otherwise writes nothing.
/// - A position beyond the size is reset to the start before writing.
///   MemoryWriteFile's `seek` never moves past the size, so this is a
///   safety net.
/// - When the write would pass the capacity, the capacity becomes twice
///   the needed size plus 4 KB, and the old buffer and the new capacity
///   are passed to an allocation helper whose result replaces the buffer
///   pointer. The helper's identity, and what it returns on failure, are
///   not established. BFME 1 calls the C runtime's `realloc` at that
///   point.
/// - Copies the bytes, advances the position, and raises the size if the
///   position passed it.
///
/// \param buffer  Bytes to copy. May be null only when `bytes` is 0.
/// \param bytes   Number of bytes to copy.
/// \return `bytes` on success; 0 for an empty write; -1 for a negative
///         count, a null buffer with a positive count, or when the buffer
///         pointer is null after the growth step (which happens only when
///         the allocation helper has returned null, on this write or an
///         earlier one).
///
/// \note BFME 1's matched body has only the first two checks: no early
///       return for an empty write, no position reset and no check of the
///       reallocation result. In the WorldBuilder debug build the two error
///       checks, the position check and the reallocation check are also
///       assertions.
/// \warning If the allocation helper returns null, the pointer to what had
///          been written is overwritten and lost. The capacity has already
///          been raised by then, so later non-empty writes that fit within
///          it return -1; a larger one asks the helper again, starting from
///          no buffer, so even if that succeeds the earlier contents are
///          gone.
/// \par Remastering
/// The capacity is computed in 32-bit signed arithmetic, so doubling
/// overflows once the needed size passes about 1 GB.
/// \see MemoryWriteFile::MemoryWriteFile


/// \fn File *createMemoryReadFile(char *data, Int size)
/// \brief Wraps a block of memory in a new MemoryReadFile, so that code
///        written for File can read it.
///
/// The read side of the memory-file pair (see the group page). Its
/// callers include ConnectionManager::processHero, which reads
/// Create-a-Hero data out of a network command, and two functions of the
/// GameSpy persistent-storage code that read a player's statistics. Each
/// hands the file to an Xfer loader and then closes it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Refuses a null pointer with a non-zero size. Otherwise it allocates a
/// MemoryReadFile over the block. That constructor copies nothing: it
/// keeps the pointer and the size, starts at position 0, and marks the file
/// open with `READ` and `BINARY` access under the name `<MemoryReadFile>`.
/// A null pointer with size 0 gives an empty file.
///
/// \param data  The bytes to read. Not copied; the caller keeps ownership
///              and must keep the block alive while the file is in use.
/// \param size  Number of bytes in the block.
/// \return The new file, or null when `data` is null and `size` is not 0.
///
/// \note The delete-on-close flag is not set, so File::close does not
///       destroy the returned object.
/// \note The name is the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction's. In the WorldBuilder
///       debug build the assert in the matching function names it `Open`,
///       qualified by the MemoryReadFile class, with parameters `buffer`
///       and `numBytes`; the original was probably a static member function
///       of MemoryReadFile.
/// \see MemoryWriteFile::write
